@*
    The dead-letter concern: the strict-mode ingest queue. When a tree's policy
    (or version config) runs strict, a non-compliant ingested item is diverted
    here instead of being applied. Read-only, and loaded on an explicit action
    because the queue can be large and is not needed to understand the tree's
    governance state.

    The queue is the area's widest surface, so it renders through the design
    system's adaptive table: a table at medium and expanded, a card list at
    compact, from one column declaration and with no media query of its own.
*@

<div class="lx-schema-grid">
    <div class="lx-schema-formcol">
        <h3 class="lx-schema-title">Dead letters</h3>
        <p class="lx-schema-note">Strict-mode ingest diverts non-compliant items here instead of applying them. Read-only.</p>
        <button type="button" class="lx-schema-btn"
                disabled="@(!Session.Can(SchemaCapability.ViewDeadLetters))"
                @onclick="LoadAsync">Load dead letters</button>
    </div>
    <div class="lx-schema-formcol">
        @if (View is not { } view)
        {
            <p class="lx-schema-empty">Select a tree, then load its dead letters.</p>
        }
        else if (!view.IsSuccess)
        {
            <p class="lx-schema-result @SchemaStatusStyles.For(view.Status)" role="status">@view.Message</p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p class="lx-schema-sub">Total: @view.Count (showing @view.Entries.Count)</p>
            <LatticeAdaptiveTable TItem="LatticeSchemaDeadLetterEntry"
                                  Items="view.Entries"
                                  Columns="EntryColumns"
                                  Label="Dead letters"
                                  EmptyText="No dead letters." />
        }
    </div>
</div>
